    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d334">

                <pb facs="0407" n="305" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg305"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6255: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin (Lockhart) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-05-08T16:00:00+08:00">May 8, 1933—4 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 8—7:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Chinese forces have retreated to Changli and Japanese-Manchukuo forces have
                    advanced to Peitaiho. There is every probability that Chinese will again be
                    compelled to withdraw to west side of Lwan River.</p>
                <p>From a reliable source I learn that through intervention of third party General
                    Ho Chu-kuo had consented to meet with Japanese on Sunday<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d334-f1"/> to discuss ways and means of ending trouble in area east
                    of Lwanchow but Japanese refused to confer. It is difficult to predict the
                    objective of the new operations but it would not be surprising if territory up
                    to the Lwan River is added to that already under control of Manchukuo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
